{"skill":{"slug":"tencentcloud-lighthouse-skill","displayName":"Tencent Cloud Lighthouse"},"version":{"version":"1.0.2","createdAt":1778230746217,"changelog":"No functional or documentation changes detected for version 1.0.2.\n\n- No file modifications present compared to the previous version.\n- SKILL.md and all relevant documentation remain unchanged.\n- No new features, bug fixes, or behavioral adjustments included in this release.","changelogSource":"user","license":"MIT-0","files":[{"path":"README.md","size":974,"sha256":"4cd93c2c0b076808896336bf850c8236922499114df79bf1f0440beb2a00461d","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":8827,"sha256":"1712e7a1780024f27814b526efefa4cf8700a43339ce4c1e9567696f0fd37205","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"script/whoami.sh","size":590,"sha256":"ae144795d0e08d3dd88229117875886ebb970cd31e080a60194bca5589a63835","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"script/tccli-oauth-helper.sh","size":16796,"sha256":"f22f6b1d4587903a4142d4326fc97bab441f8f6faa247e97fa0279ee1498ef41","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"references/traffic-package.md","size":931,"sha256":"eefd63b57613c02b965969c295c6e7d29775f82b3aac269c02a5431f838f9a52","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/remote-command-tat.md","size":1760,"sha256":"f07372e0063d142e505bf322fdd0f4004c5776c06d10625756d0ac74c565757c","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/application-deployment.md","size":5622,"sha256":"001d4f56f65b04cd051081200139447507421bb0fe39fe3fdb5b9e0bbdabada0","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/firewall-management.md","size":1731,"sha256":"8ea35c978064e9a400f9046e68ae5f97dc6e158f520a41579d50183b8b0e514c","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/monitoring-alerting.md","size":1960,"sha256":"13e825f2b1d2cbb9e826f055ebce10595f717c09eea4b97df7eda4ccacc7376b","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/instance-management.md","size":2532,"sha256":"fded9b058701a261ffa94520ae8d51c6a77b82b716648bcbd64bfdd90d255610","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/snapshot-blueprint.md","size":1839,"sha256":"342154faea3307c72a4e964e49bf48c304be09e30a81565fda2a218082fa5640","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2813,"sha256":"b5569e7f821ace5c129cd1787354d5c3f299fd95c70ce67dad769cc8c9acf688","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789060579087,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"c2525dfac8c84158c5d245eab18fb699416a9debb0888199c31a1c79fc32dbba","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/c2525dfac8c84158c5d245eab18fb699416a9debb0888199c31a1c79fc32dbba","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: tencentcloud-lighthouse-skill\nVersion: 1.0.2\n\nThe skill bundle provides a legitimate interface for managing Tencent Cloud Lighthouse instances using the official 'tccli' command-line tool. It includes helper scripts for OAuth authentication (script/tccli-oauth-helper.sh) and instance identification (script/whoami.sh) that interact with standard Tencent Cloud endpoints and metadata services. The instructions in SKILL.md and the reference documents are well-structured, providing clear workflows for cloud management tasks while incorporating safety guidelines for high-risk operations.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779163106547},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":38,"checkedAt":1789060579087},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This skill is mostly aligned with Tencent Lighthouse administration, but it handles cloud credentials and remote server changes in ways that deserve careful review before installation.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Remote deployment guidance includes TAT shell execution, `curl | sudo bash`, Docker service enablement, unpinned Docker images, `npm install`, and firewall rules open to `0.0.0.0/0`. These actions fit deployment work but are overbroad without explicit scoping and confirmation.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Installation uses an unpinned `pip install tccli` package. This is normal for a CLI-based cloud skill but should ideally be pinned or installed in an isolated environment.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The skill includes broad triggers like `instance ID` and `whoami`, directs agents to create a new instance by default for ambiguous create/deploy/setup requests, and provides mutating workflows where confirmation guidance is inconsistent or separated from the risky command.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The OAuth helper writes SecretId, SecretKey, session token, access token, and refresh token under `~/.tccli` without enforcing restrictive permissions, accepts OAuth state mismatches, and allows profile names to influence credential file paths.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated Lighthouse management purpose matches most capabilities, including tccli use, OAuth setup, instance management, firewall changes, TAT commands, snapshots, and deployment. The concern is not purpose mismatch but high-impact cloud authority combined with unsafe defaults such as creating new instances by default for deploy/setup requests.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Install only if you are comfortable giving the agent real Tencent Cloud authority. Do not paste OAuth verification codes into third-party chats, restrict credential files to your user account, confirm every billable or mutating action, avoid the `curl | sudo bash` deployment pattern, pin packages and Docker images, and replace broad `0.0.0.0/0` firewall rules with the narrowest CIDR that works.","findings":"[TP4] expected: Credential setup is disclosed in SKILL.md and README.md and is coherent for a cloud-management skill, so I downgraded the claimed purpose mismatch; the implementation still has credential-handling concerns.\n[AE1] unexpected: The referenced OAuth helper was present locally and was fully inspected during this review, so the partial-inspection concern does not remain as an artifact issue.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: The helper tells users they may send the OAuth verification code to an AI assistant, even though the decoded code contains authentication material. This is materially unsafe for a cloud-auth workflow.\n[SSD-3] unexpected: The credential-sharing concern is accepted: the artifact text encourages handing OAuth material to an assistant instead of warning that it is sensitive.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: Defaulting to new billable instance creation for create/deploy/setup requests, and opening application ports broadly, are not sufficiently scoped by adjacent confirmation and cost/exposure warnings.\n[TM2] unexpected: Some command chaining is ordinary diagnostics, but the deployment guide's `curl` into privileged shell and chained privileged setup commands are unsafe examples for remote server administration.\n[PE2] expected: Privilege elevation is often needed for runtime and Docker installation, but the examples use broad root-level operations without validation or tight scoping.\n[RA2] expected: Docker enablement, `nohup`, and restart policies are expected for application deployment, so I do not treat them as rogue-agent persistence; they still should be user-approved operational changes.\n[RP1] unexpected: Using placeholder or implicit-latest Docker images is not necessary for the skill purpose and creates avoidable supply-chain risk.\n[LP3] expected: Shell, network, and cloud CLI access are expected for this skill, but the artifact does not define a narrow allowed-tool boundary for read-only versus mutating actions.\n[SQP-1] unexpected: The broad activation phrases could load the skill outside a clear Lighthouse context, which matters because the skill can prompt for credentials and perform cloud operations.\n[T03] unexpected: The static finding is accepted: the deployment reference runs a remote NodeSource setup script through a privileged shell on the target instance.\n[T09] unexpected: The static credential findings are accepted: credential files are written without enforced restrictive permissions, OAuth state mismatch does not abort, profile names are not constrained, and public firewall exposure is used as a standard example.\n[T08] expected: Installing tccli and application dependencies is expected, but unpinned dependency resolution should be treated as a review item rather than evidence of malicious intent.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789060579087}}}}}